From c2c4279a962366602b95e04afef9ee4ed8662adc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: paulkoan <paulkoan@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 19:51:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Linux: the four MSVC-only fixes, the full PTX list, and a
 `serve` mode that speaks our worker protocol

gcc 14 / glslang 16.6.0 build the whole thing: 13/13 shaders to SPIR-V, all 78 PTX
kernels, a dlss5vk that runs the network on the GPU box (9.960 ms median at 2560x1440
on a 4080 SUPER, 249 dispatches, PTX route).

Four fixes, all of them things MSVC accepted:

  * src/kernels.h: `= Chain()` as a default argument constructs a nested type with
    default member initializers while that type is still incomplete. Declared a
    static default-constructed Chain instead; same thing to every caller.
  * src/vk_context.h, src/nr_model.h: memcpy without <cstring>.
  * scripts/build_linux.sh: VK_ENABLE_BETA_EXTENSIONS must reach volk.c, or volk.h
    never declares the VK_NV_cuda_kernel_launch entry points (five undefined
    references at link). The PowerShell build gets the define for free by compiling
    volk.c in the same cl invocation.

And the bug that replaced one of them: the script's PTX list is a hand copy of the
emitter invocations in scripts/build_shaders.ps1, and it was copied from an incomplete
read of that file - the last two loops were missing, so 12 of 78 kernels were never
generated: the five fused 32-channel block variants (block32_e4m3_f2/66/74/130/48) and
the seven split-512 GEMMs (gemmv_e4m3_K{512,256,4096,1024}_f*_s1_m96). Nothing failed at
build time - the host resolves kernel files by name when a dispatch is first reached -
so it surfaced as

    error: cannot read PTX kernel ./build/shaders/../ptx/block32_e4m3_f74.ptx

The script now emits all 78 and checks the count, because the failure mode of a short
list is a run-time error that names the missing file and not the reason.

New: `dlss5vk serve`, so the pipeline can run the pass without Wine.

It speaks the wire format native/dlss5-feed-host64.cpp speaks, byte for byte, so
pointing NS_WORKER_CMD at it is the whole integration - the startup header, FRAME_MAGIC
with colour and motion inline, OUT_MAGIC with the pixels back. No upscaling (the header's
full size must be 0 or the work size), no shared-memory channel (SHMI is refused with
ok=0, the documented fallback). The history is kept: serve expands the frame it published
last into a proxy of its own and hands it to preprocess.comp, which samples it for lanes
7-9 where the demo's nr_preprocess.comp samples its own history surface. With the zero
motion field the pipeline sends the reprojection is the identity, so that sample is the
pixel itself rather than a motion-compensated one, and one extra dispatch - no host bytes
moved - is the whole cost. A frame that carries a reset, or one served in bypass, has no
history and falls back to the current proxy, the first-frame input. The composite is
still the no-history one: it does not blend the history, so what changed here is the
network's input, and nothing claims the picture is better.

With FRAME_FLAG_BYPASS the pixels come back untouched and no device is created, which is
what lets the framing, the header parse, the refusals and the end-of-stream handshake be
tested on a machine with no GPU.

Also lands: `--model`/`--shaders`/`--frames` for the mode, the warm record that pays the
249-kernel JIT burst on a zero proxy where the log can say what the wait was, and a
per-frame timing line on stderr.
